---
# FLUX, WATCHED BECAUSE IT IS NOW THE DEPLOY PATH.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THESE RULES ARE ACTUALLY FOR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A deploy used to be a CI job, and a broken deploy was a red job in a list somebody reads. It is
# now four controllers agreeing: source-controller fetches the commit, image-reflector-controller
# scans the registry, image-automation-controller commits the winning tag, kustomize-controller
# applies it. Any of them can stop WITHOUT ANYTHING GOING RED, because a custom resource turning
# `Ready=False` is not an event that reaches a human.
#
# The failure that produces is not an outage. It is the cluster serving the last image it happened
# to receive, forever, while every CI run stays green -- the same silent staleness that caused the
# 2026-09-03 outage and put Flux here to begin with. Replacing a loud failure with a quiet one
# would have been a downgrade, so these rules exist as a condition of retiring `kubectl set image`
# rather than as a follow-up to it.
#
# ⚠️ `gotk_reconcile_condition` IS EMITTED FOR EVERY STATUS VALUE, one series per (kind, name,
# type, status) with a 0/1 value -- so the series EXISTING says nothing and only `== 1` is a fact.
# An expression that omits it fires the moment Flux reports anything at all, which is the shape
# that paged on a healthy replica set (see the alert-expression memory) and the reason
# infra/test/alert-rules/flux.yml asserts the healthy case stays silent.
groups:
  - name: flux
    rules:
      # THE WHOLE DEPLOY PATH, ONE ALERT. Kind-agnostic on purpose: a GitRepository that cannot
      # fetch, a Kustomization that cannot apply and an ImagePolicy that matches nothing all end
      # with new commits not reaching the cluster, and all of them show up here.
      - alert: FluxReconciliationFailing
        expr: gotk_reconcile_condition{type="Ready",status="False"} == 1
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.kind }}/{{ $labels.name }} has been NotReady for 30m"
          description: >-
            This object has failed to reconcile for half an hour, so whatever it owns is now
            whatever it was last able to apply. Start with
            `kubectl -n flux-system describe {{ $labels.kind }} {{ $labels.name }}` -- the message
            on the Ready condition names the cause directly, and the controller's log
            (`kubectl -n flux-system logs deploy/<controller>`) has the detail.

      # THE IMAGE HALF, SEPARATELY AND LOUDER, because its failure is the invisible one. The other
      # kinds fail against a manifest somebody just changed and is watching; this one fails against
      # a registry, silently, when nobody is looking at it at all.
      - alert: FluxDeployPathStalled
        expr: >-
          gotk_reconcile_condition{type="Ready",status="False",
            kind=~"ImageRepository|ImagePolicy|ImageUpdateAutomation"} == 1
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "new builds are no longer reaching the cluster ({{ $labels.kind }}/{{ $labels.name }})"
          description: >-
            CI can still build and push images, and nothing in CI will go red, but none of them
            become deploys while this is failing. The three usual causes, in the order worth
            checking: the ImagePolicy matches no tag (compare the pattern against
            `main-<utc14>-<sha7>` and remember FluxImageAutomationSpec guards exactly this), the
            registry scan is failing, or the deploy key that lets image-automation PUSH has been
            revoked -- that key is the only credential in this path and it is repo-scoped, id
            162222850.

      # SUSPENDED IS NOT AN ERROR AND THAT IS THE PROBLEM. A suspended automation reports itself
      # perfectly healthy while deploys quietly stop. Two hours is long enough that a deliberate
      # pause during an incident does not page, and short enough that a forgotten one does.
      - alert: FluxSuspended
        expr: gotk_suspend_status == 1
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.kind }}/{{ $labels.name }} has been suspended for 2h"
          description: >-
            A suspended object is not reconciled and not deployed, and reports no error for it. If
            this was a deliberate pause, resume it; if nobody remembers doing it, treat it as an
            unexplained stop to the deploy path. Note that suspending by `kubectl patch` DOES NOT
            STICK here -- the Kustomization reverts it from git within minutes -- so a suspension
            that persists this long was committed, and resuming means a commit too.

      # THE EXPORTER ITSELF. Without this the three rules above go quiet exactly when the thing
      # they watch dies hardest, because an absent series cannot compare `== 1`.
      - alert: FluxControllerDown
        expr: up{job="flux"} == 0
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.controller }} is not answering its metrics port"
          description: >-
            Either the controller is down or its NodePort no longer matches. The ports are fixed in
            infra/kubernetes/flux-metrics/services.yaml and must agree with
            `fleet.prometheus.fluxTargets`; nothing checks that, and a disagreement looks exactly
            like this. Check `kubectl -n flux-system get pods` first -- if they are Running, it is
            the port.
